From 4c1bd8c6a2b99c29b71b1df8af9e1ebec733aff7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:15:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 06/27] allwinner: h713: an EL3 service that hands the receiver
 its HDCP 1.4 key set

The projector's HDCP 1.4 key set is chip-bound ciphertext in its own secure storage.
Only the Crypto Engine can open it, with a key that exists nowhere software can
reach, and it writes the result straight into the receiver's key sink at
`0x03041400`. This adds the one service that starts that transfer, so the boot
loader can put the key in place before the display firmware polls for it.

Three measured rules shape it, and none may be "simplified" away:

  - key-select 3 towards readable DRAM is refused with ESR 0x11. That is the
    design, not a fault: such a task would be key export.
  - the DMA source must lie inside the Trusted OS window, so the caller stages the
    bytes there first. This was the one thing missing for two days, because the
    engine answers the same refusal either way - which is also why an eFuse flag
    reading zero looked like the cause and was not.
  - the transfer has to happen after the TV domain is powered. Before that it
    completes with ESR 0 and latches nothing.

`SUNXI_CE_NS_BASE`, `SUNXI_CE_S_BASE` and `SUNXI_HDCP14_SINK` are the engine's two
banks and the sink. `sunxi_security.c` gains this board's own vendor SPC sequence
behind `H713_VENDOR_SECURITY_SETUP`, register for register: the mainline sequence
assumes no secure OS, this platform has one, and without the vendor's version the
engine's secure bank is not reachable at all.

The workbench probes - engine tasks with caller-chosen key slots, lengths and
addresses, descriptor dumps, eFuse reads - are compiled only with
`H713_HDCP_RESEARCH=1` and are absent from a release build (41076 bytes against
53372, no probe strings left). A shipped image carries two services: the version
call and the one above. What stays reachable is bounded on purpose: one source
address inside the protected window, a fixed length, and the destination is always
the sink. The worst a caller can do is install a key set that does not
authenticate - it can never read one back.

`MAX_XLAT_TABLES` goes from 4 to 8 because the service maps one scratch page
dynamically; 4 was exactly the static need. `platform_def.h` also carries the BL32
window of the payload experiment that follows in the next commit - one file, two
topics, and splitting the hunks would have cost more than it explains.

Proven at the picture with a cleared sink as the control, because the sink survives
a warm reset: wipe -> the source reports Desired -> push -> the source reports
Enabled. Three such pairs, the last with the image that would ship.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
